1. Link-preview tags (Open Graph/Twitter). When a trip link is pasted into WhatsApp or iMessage, it would show the cover photo, title and date range. It’s a few lines in the shared page head, and every template benefits.

2. Per-trip template override. A "template" key in a trip’s trip_content.json, so a road trip can be cinema and a family album scrapbook. The library page could then show a picker on each trip card as well. The main catch is that a live server and a static library each serve one stylesheet today, so assets would need to be per template.

3. Print or PDF “photo book” view. A print stylesheet for scrapbook and journal that produces clean pages (one day per page, with map and story). People often want a physical or PDF keepsake, and the scrapbook look suits it.

4. Steps within a day. Split a day into several steps by place or by gaps in time (“Morning in Florence”, “Evening at Piazzale Michelangelo”), each with its own text and map pin. The journal template already has the timeline layout for this.

5. Elevation and route extras. The GPX data would give an elevation profile on day pages, plus ascent, descent and speed. The library page could show a “countries visited” stat.

